The Security Council,

Recalling all of its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in the Central African Republic, in particular resolution 2745 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Central African Republic, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,

Welcoming the efforts made by the Government of the Central African Republic, in cooperation with the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA) and other bilateral and international partners, to advance security sector reform, disarmament, demobilization, reintegration and repatriation (DDRR), and the progressive restoration and extension of state authority throughout the national territory,

Welcoming further the continued commitment to the implementation of the Political Agreement for Peace and Reconciliation in the Central African Republic (APPR-CA) and the Luanda Roadmap, and urging all armed groups to cease hostilities immediately and unconditionally, lay down their weapons, and engage constructively in the peace process,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks, human rights abuses and violations of international humanitarian law committed by armed groups and associated individuals, including attacks directed against civilians, United Nations personnel, and humanitarian workers,

Expressing concern over the illicit exploitation and trafficking of natural resources, as well as the illicit trafficking of small arms and light weapons, which continue to fund and fuel the activities of armed groups and destabilize the country and the wider region,

Taking note of the reports of the Panel of Experts on the Central African Republic, and acknowledging the importance of its work in supporting the sanctions committee,

Determining that the situation in the Central African Republic continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 31 July 2026 the measures concerning the arms embargo targeting armed groups and associated individuals operating in the Central African Republic, as set out in resolution 2745 (2024);

2. Reaffirms that the measures referred to in paragraph 1 shall not apply to the supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel, or the provision of assistance, advice or training, to the Central African Republic security forces, including state civilian law enforcement institutions;

3. Decides to renew until 31 July 2026 the travel ban and asset freeze measures established pursuant to resolution 2399 (2018) and extended by resolution 2745 (2024);

4. Recalls resolution 2664 (2022) and affirms that the humanitarian exemption provisions set out therein shall continue to apply to the measures renewed by this resolution;

5. Decides to extend until 31 August 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts as established pursuant to resolution 2399 (2018) and extended by resolution 2745 (2024), expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding its further extension no later than 31 July 2026, and requests the Secretary-General to take the necessary administrative measures as expeditiously as possible to support the Panel’s activities;

6. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a midterm report no later than 31 January 2026, a final report no later than 31 May 2026, and progress updates to the Committee as appropriate;

7. Urges all parties and all Member States, particularly those in the region, as well as international, regional and subregional organizations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts and the safety of its members, and further urges all Member States and relevant United Nations bodies to ensure unhindered access for the Panel of Experts to execute its mandate;

8. Expresses its intention to keep the situation in the Central African Republic under continuous review and to be prepared to review the appropriateness of the measures contained in this resolution at any time, in light of the evolution of the security situation in the country and progress achieved in the peace and stabilization process;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling all of its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in the Central African Republic, in particular resolution 2745 (2024),

