2. Reaffirms that the measures referred to in paragraph 1 shall not apply to the supply, sale or transfer of arms and related materiel, or the provision of assistance, advice or training, to the Central African Republic security forces, including state civilian law enforcement institutions;

3. Encourages Member States undertaking seizures of arms, ammunition, or related materiel destined for armed groups in violation of the targeted arms embargo to report such seizures promptly to the Committee and coordinate with the Committee and the Government of the Central African Republic regarding the appropriate tracing and disposal of seized items;

4. Decides to renew until 31 July 2026 the travel ban and asset freeze measures established pursuant to resolution 2399 (2018) and extended by resolution 2745 (2024);

5. Recalls resolution 2664 (2022) and affirms that the humanitarian exemption provisions set out therein shall continue to apply to the measures renewed by this resolution;

6. Decides to extend until 31 August 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts as established pursuant to resolution 2399 (2018) and extended by resolution 2745 (2024), expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding its further extension no later than 31 July 2026, requests the Secretary-General to take the necessary administrative measures as expeditiously as possible to support the Panel’s activities, and stresses that the Panel of Experts should gather information from reliable, independently verified sources and carry out its investigations in a strictly objective, transparent, and impartial manner;

7. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a midterm report no later than 31 January 2026, a final report no later than 31 May 2026, and progress updates to the Committee as appropriate;

8. Urges all parties and all Member States, particularly those in the region, as well as international, regional and subregional organizations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts and the safety of its members, and further urges all Member States and relevant United Nations bodies to ensure unhindered access for the Panel of Experts to execute its mandate, in close cooperation and consultation with the Government of the Central African Republic;

9. Expresses its intention to keep the situation in the Central African Republic under continuous review and to be prepared to review the appropriateness of the measures contained in this resolution at any time, including through the possible suspension, easing or lifting of measures, in light of the evolution of the security situation in the country and progress achieved in the peace and stabilization process;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

